= Practical chemistry
{scope}
{title2=No. II.}

Original volume: <Examination papers for the Degree of Bachelor of Medicine by the University of Oxford (1864–1867)>, PDF pages 31–31. https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3e/Examination_papers_for_the_Degree_of_Bachelor_of_medicine_-_1864-67_(IA_b24757810).pdf#page=31[Open the original paper in the scanned volume]. Oxford, December, 1865.

Original questions are transcribed from the public-domain edition. Credit: Wellcome Collection; original supplied by Royal College of Physicians, London. https://wellcomecollection.org/works/d7b9zgnx[Source and public-domain rights statement].

== 1
{scope}

**Original question.**

Each of the tubes, $a$, $b$, $c$, $d$, contains a single substance.

=== Solution

TODO: The single substances in tubes a, b, c and d are absent and no identifying test observations are given.

**The identities of a, b, c and d remain undetermined.** The assertion that each tube holds a single <chemical substance> narrows the type of problem but gives no <chemical test> result from which to choose a name.

A useful examination would give each specimen its own labelled record and preserve an untouched portion. Record physical appearance and behaviour in suitable <solvents> before choosing tests. A soluble inorganic <salt> calls for separate investigation of its <cation> and <anion>; an insoluble material requires a justified preparation step before solution tests can be interpreted. A colour change, <precipitate> or evolved <gas> must be recorded as an observation before it is used as evidence for a composition. Fresh portions prevent one <reagent> from introducing the ion sought by the next test.

In <qualitative chemical analysis>, a discriminating result is followed by an independent confirmation and comparison with a <reagent blank> and a known reference. For example, the behaviour of a metal <hydroxide> depends on both the <cation> and the chemical conditions; “a precipitate formed” is not a complete identification.<Testing Salts for Anions and Cations by the Royal Society of Chemistry>[Metal hydroxides and negative-ion tests: colour, behaviour in excess reagent and separate anion tests]{ref} The record supplied here contains none of those observations, so no particular reaction or identity can be assigned to any of the four letters.

== 2
{scope}

**Original question.**

The solution $e$ is to be examined for hydrocyanic acid, and the solution $f$ for iron and magnesia.

=== Solution

TODO: Solutions e and f and their test observations are absent. Hydrocyanic acid, iron and magnesia are testing targets, not supplied positive findings; general test methods can still be described.

**The presence or absence of <hydrogen cyanide> in e, and of <iron> or <magnesium> in f, remains unestablished.** The named substances are targets of the examination. They are not positive findings.

The period name “hydrocyanic acid” refers to aqueous <hydrogen cyanide>. “Magnesia” is the historical name for <magnesium oxide>; examination of a <chemical solution> for magnesia normally concerns dissolved <magnesium> compounds, rather than a claim that intact <magnesium oxide> particles are dissolved unchanged. The modern <ionic equations> below express test principles and are not observations made on f.

For e, the analytical task is to obtain a response attributable to <cyanide>, distinguish it from responses of other constituents, and check the entire procedure against a <reagent blank> and a known reference. The distinction between free <cyanide> and <cyanide> held in a <coordination complex> matters: a test for one fraction need not measure the other. As a modern comparison, the <Cyanide in Waters and Extracts, Method 9014 by United States Environmental Protection Agency> provides <titrimetric analysis> and <spectrophotometric analysis>, with specified sample preparation, interferences and quality controls. This 2014 procedure illustrates how a result is validated; it is not being attributed to the 1865 candidate.<Cyanide in Waters and Extracts, Method 9014 by United States Environmental Protection Agency>[§§1–2: analytical scope and measurement principles; §§4 and 9: interferences and controls]{ref} No numerical <concentration>, positive colour or negative result can be extracted from the label e.

An odour is neither an acceptable identifying experiment nor a reliable negative test. <Hydrogen cyanide> is volatile and highly toxic, and many people cannot detect its supposed characteristic smell. Any actual analysis belongs in a suitably contained laboratory method; uncontrolled acidification of a possible <cyanide> solution can release <hydrogen cyanide>.<Hydrogen cyanide emergency response information by NIOSH>[Agent Characteristics: volatility, toxicity and unreliable odour warning]{ref}<Cyanide in Waters and Extracts, Method 9014 by United States Environmental Protection Agency>[§5.2: hydrogen cyanide release on acidification]{ref}

For f, distinguish <iron(II)> from <iron(III)> when describing a test. On suitable separate portions, <hydroxide> can produce a green <iron(II) hydroxide> <precipitate> or a brown hydrated <iron(III) hydroxide> <precipitate>. <Iron(II)> products can oxidise during handling, so the time at which a colour is observed matters. The simplified precipitation balances are:
$$
\mathrm{Fe^{2+}+2OH^-\longrightarrow Fe(OH)_2(s)},\qquad
\mathrm{Fe^{3+}+3OH^-\longrightarrow Fe(OH)_3(s)}.
$$
These equations describe possible chemical responses, not a choice between them for the missing f. Tests intended for a particular <oxidation state> cannot be treated as universal tests of every form of <iron>.

Dissolved <magnesium> can give a white <magnesium hydroxide> <precipitate>:
$$
\mathrm{Mg^{2+}+2OH^-\longrightarrow Mg(OH)_2(s)}.
$$
The white solid does not characteristically dissolve in excess <sodium hydroxide> under the usual qualitative-test conditions. This differentiates it from some amphoteric <hydroxides>, but is not uniquely specific for <magnesium>.<Testing Salts for Anions and Cations by the Royal Society of Chemistry>[Metal hydroxides: iron(II), iron(III), magnesium and the distinction from aluminium in excess sodium hydroxide]{ref} If <iron> and <magnesium> were together, a single mixed <precipitate> could obscure their separate behaviour. An analytical separation with controlled <pH>, followed by confirmation on the separated fractions, would be needed; indiscriminately adding excess alkali is not proof of separation.<Qualitative Analysis of Metal Ions in Solution by Williams College>[printed II & III-15–17: hydroxide concentration, magnesium interference and iron confirmation]{ref} The actual <oxidation state> of any <iron>, the presence of <magnesium>, and the outcome of such a separation all remain unknown.

== 3
{scope}

**Original question.**

Under what circumstances can you obtain a fixed gas from the solid substance $g$, and what is the nature of the gas ?

=== Solution

TODO: Solid substance g is an unidentified supplied specimen. Without its identity or relevant observations, the particular gas and conditions for obtaining it cannot be determined.

**Neither the gas nor the conditions required to obtain it from g can be identified.** The word “fixed” does not supply a chemical formula, and the existence of a labelled solid does not establish that it is a <carbonate> or any other particular <chemical compound>.

A defensible investigation would first characterise g and distinguish physical release of an absorbed <gas> from a <chemical reaction> that produces a new <gas>. Whether <heat transfer>[heating], contact with a <solvent>, or a selected <reagent> is relevant depends on that characterisation. An arbitrary <thermal decomposition> equation would therefore assume the very composition that is missing.

If an identified reaction produced a <gas>, the apparatus would be chosen for its actual properties: a reaction vessel and delivery system, a means of distinguishing the product from initially enclosed <air>, and a compatible collection or detection method. Collection over <water> would be unsuitable when dissolution or reaction with <water> materially changes the product; a <gas syringe> is an alternative only when compatible with the substances and conditions. The gas would then require discriminating <chemical tests>, and the residue could help check the proposed reaction. No evolution, odour, combustion behaviour, <precipitation> result or residue is reported for g, so none of those observations can be supplied as its answer.
